---
title: Sheet-pan dinner
description: Lesson 2.4. The module together, on one tray, with ingredients you choose.
servings: 2
time: 1 hour
tags: [cooking-course, the-oven]
lesson: "2.4"
technique: staggered roasting
status: draft
---

> Technique: staggered roasting. Everything shares one tray and one
> oven, but not one cooking time. The ingredients are slots: pick what
> you have for each one, keep the amounts, and put each on the tray
> when its time comes.

Heat the oven to 220 °C (200 °C fan).

Season @chicken thighs{4}(bone-in, skin-on; or 6 sausages) with @salt{1%tsp}. Cut @hard vegetables{400%g}(potato, carrot, parsnip or squash) into 2 cm pieces and @soft vegetables{300%g}(pepper, courgette, red onion or cherry tomatoes) into pieces about twice that size, keeping the two groups apart.

Toss the hard vegetables with half of the @oil{2%tbsp}(sunflower or rapeseed) and a pinch of salt. Spread them on a #large baking tray{} in one layer and set the chicken on the tray between them, skin side up. Roast for ~{25%minutes}.

> Why in stages: dense vegetables can take much more cooking than
> tender ones. Start them all together and the soft ones are finished
> long before the hard ones.
-- source: "7 Tips for Successful Soup Making", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-10-10, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/6226-7-tips-for-successful-soup-making (the same rule, stated for soup)

Toss the soft vegetables with the rest of the oil and add them to the tray, in the gaps. Roast for ~{20%minutes} more, until the vegetables are browned at the edges and the thickest part of a thigh, away from the bone, reads at least 74 °C on a #thermometer{}.

> Why space: pieces packed together trap steam, and steam stops
> browning. If the tray is full, use two.
-- source: Liz Bomze, Keith Dresser and Emily Rahravan, "How to Roast Any Vegetable", America's Test Kitchen, 2024-02-12, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/8018-how-to-roast-any-vegetable

> Safety: 74 °C is the safe minimum for poultry, read before the tray
> leaves the oven.
-- source: USDA Food Safety and Inspection Service, "Safe Minimum Internal Temperature Chart", last updated 2025-04-14 (read from an archive copy), https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/safe-temperature-chart : all poultry 165 °F (73.9 °C)

> Science: this is the rule from lesson 2.1 with meat added. A wet
> surface is held near 100 °C until its water has gone; only then can
> it climb towards oven temperature and brown. Look up: Maillard
> reaction, latent heat of vaporization.
-- source: Harold McGee, On Food and Cooking, 2nd ed., Scribner, 2004, section "High Temperatures and Dry Cooking Methods", https://app.ckbk.com/reference/food00011c14s001e001se003/high-temperatures-and-dry-cooking-methods

Rest the tray for ~{5%minutes} before serving.

> Next time, without the recipe: one hot oven, one tray, and a list of
> what takes longest. Start with that, add the rest in order, keep the
> pieces apart, and stop when the meat reaches its temperature.
